---
/**
 * The landing page.
 *
 * Deliberately outside the Starlight docs collection: `/features` and `/guides`
 * keep Starlight's chrome, and `src/pages/` takes route priority over
 * Starlight's injected routes, so this owns `/` with no collision.
 *
 * House rule for the copy here: one idea per band, a statement headline, at
 * most three short paragraphs, one visual. Every band ends with a link to the
 * docs page that carries the detail — if a paragraph is explaining lane
 * semantics or flag behaviour, it belongs there, not here.
 */
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import Faq from '../components/landing/Faq.astro';

const title = 'maiTerm — keep a window full of coding agents on track';
const description =
  'A terminal for running Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity and your shells side by side: a kanban board shared with your agents, a rules-driven supervisor watching the window, and every tab back exactly as it was after a restart.';

/**
 * The hero rail: this page's own table of contents, newest feature first.
 *
 * **Every row is one band on this page, in page order.** That rule is what keeps
 * the rail honest — a row cannot outlive the thing it names, because adding one
 * means adding an `id` to a band and removing one means the anchor breaks in an
 * obvious way. Do not list a feature here that has no band to land on; if it is
 * worth the rail it is worth a band, and if it is not, it belongs in EVERYTHING.
 *
 * The first entry is the SLOT: each release that ships something worth leading
 * with takes the top of the list and the `new` flag, and the previous holder
 * drops back into the plain rows below it. Exactly one entry carries `new` — two
 * "New" badges means neither is.
 *
 * `blurb` runs to about six words. It is a signpost, not a summary; the band it
 * points at does the explaining.
 *
 * `shot` marks the few that get a row with a blurb. On a desktop those rows are a tab
 * list for the picture beside them: pointing at one shows its screenshot, a one-line
 * `caption` and a Read more link to its band. So a row needs a REAL screenshot of the
 * feature to be one — a row that left the picture unchanged read as broken (Darryl,
 * 2026-11-27). Below 64rem the rows are plain links to their bands, which carry the
 * picture themselves. The rest are a compact run of links under the rows: the rail has to
 * END about where the shot does (fifteen full rows ran a screen and a half past it).
 *
 * On the top entry, name the RUNTIME rather than "agent". Everywhere else on this
 * site an agent IS a CLI — Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity — so "two agent logins" reads as
 * two different agents signed in, which is the one thing that feature does not
 * do. It is two accounts for the same agent.
 */
const KEY_FEATURES = [
  {
    id: 'antigravity',
    // v3.16.0. Backed by features/agents.md "Antigravity", checked against
    // src-tauri/src/claude_code/agy.rs, mcp_stdio.rs and src/lib/agents/descriptor.ts.
    // Google's Antigravity CLI (`agy`), wire key "gemini". It is NOT Gemini CLI — never say
    // "Gemini" here. No fork (supportsFork: false); Hive workers stay Claude Code only.
    title: 'Antigravity',
    blurb: 'A third agent, fully wired in',
    new: true,
  },
  {
    id: 'hive',
    // v3.13.0. Backed by features/hive.md, checked against src-tauri/src/hive/ and
    // components/hive/. Claude Code only, macOS for the full thing (job_sandbox::supported
    // refuses queued jobs elsewhere; create.rs refuses Windows) — the band note says so.
    title: 'Hive',
    blurb: 'One goal, a Queen and her crew',
  },
  {
    id: 'files',
    // v3.12.0. Backed by features/files.md, checked against src/lib/components/filetree/,
    // stores/fileTree.svelte.ts and src-tauri/src/agent_files/. "Read" is literal: Read/Grep/Glob
    // tool calls light a row (agents.ts, READ_LIGHT_MS); changes are hook evidence, never guesses.
    title: 'Files',
    blurb: 'See what every agent reads and changes',
    shot: {
      src: '/screenshots/files-column.webp',
      alt: 'The Files column between the sidebar and an editor tab: the web-ui repository on branch main, its src folder open, session.ts marked as the file in front',
      pos: 'left top',
    },
    caption: 'A project tree beside your terminals that follows the tab you are in, and marks what each agent read and changed.',
  },
  {
    id: 'browser',
    // v3.11.0. Backed by features/chrome-extension.md, checked against src-tauri/src/browser_link/
    // and chrome-extension/. "Your" browser is literal: the extension drives the human's own
    // Chrome profile through chrome.debugger — not a new headless one. Only the human links.
    title: 'Your browser, linked',
    blurb: 'Agents debug the tabs you hand them',
  },
  {
    id: 'mcp',
    // v3.7.0. Backed by features/mcp-servers.md. Names the runtimes, per the rule above: Claude
    // Code under every managed account, Codex and Antigravity locally, Claude Code and
    // Antigravity (v3.16.0) on ssh hosts. NOT Codex over ssh (docs/mcp-proxy.md §2) — don't
    // widen the blurb to "every agent".
    title: 'One MCP sign-in',
    blurb: 'Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity, every account',
  },
  {
    id: 'requests',
    // v3.5.0. Backed by features/agents.md "Organising tabs, meshes and maiTerm". "Who talks
    // to whom" is the line the app draws (docs/agent-requests.md §1); moves that don't touch
    // a mesh act at once, so don't promise that agents ask before every change.
    title: 'Agents that ask first',
    blurb: 'They tidy up; who talks to whom is yours',
  },
  {
    id: 'inside',
    // v3.4.0. Backed by features/agents.md "The maiTerm mod (Claude Code)". Claude Code
    // only, and only versions with mod support — the band says so; don't widen it to Codex.
    title: 'Inside Claude Code',
    blurb: 'Answers by meaning, never typed into your draft',
  },
  {
    id: 'loom',
    // "Without opening their terminals" is the promise and it is literal: Focus reads each
    // agent's transcript the way the phone does (get_tab_transcript), answers the prompt it
    // is stopped at off the screen, and sends through send_tab_message. It does not replace
    // the terminal — the tab is still there — so don't say "never".
    title: 'Workstream Loom',
    blurb: 'Talk to every agent from one place',
  },
  {
    id: 'accounts',
    // Not "two". There is no cap — managed_accounts is an unbounded list and every
    // active one is handed to the tabs that ask for it — so naming a number both
    // undersells the feature and invites the question of where the limit is.
    title: 'Claude Code accounts',
    // NOT "an account per client". There is no per-tab or per-workspace binding —
    // one account per runtime is active at a time (Preferences.active_account_ids)
    // and a tab reads it at spawn. Promise the simultaneity, not an assignment the
    // app does not store. No number either: managed_accounts is unbounded.
    blurb: 'As many logins as you need, side by side',
  },
  {
    id: 'stack',
    title: 'Workspace stack',
    blurb: 'One dev server, however many agents',
    shot: {
      src: '/screenshots/stack-console.webp',
      alt: "A workspace's three services as dots and a panel in the sidebar, with the web service's console open in a drawer over the terminal, showing the address it serves on",
      pos: 'left bottom',
    },
    caption: 'The services a project runs, started in tabs maiTerm owns, watched, and shown to every agent so none starts a second copy.',
  },
  {
    id: 'board',
    title: 'Task board',
    blurb: 'A real kanban board, shared with them',
    shot: {
      src: '/screenshots/tasks-panel.webp',
      alt: "The task panel beside a terminal: the task active now, then a workstream's review and blocked tasks, each with its note",
      pos: 'right top',
    },
    caption: 'Your agents keep their tasks on a board you can read and edit: workstreams, lanes, what is blocked and on whom.',
  },
  {
    id: 'overlord',
    title: 'Overlord',
    blurb: 'Rules that watch the whole window',
    shot: {
      src: '/screenshots/overlord-board.webp',
      alt: "maiTerm's Overlord deck on its Board view: workstreams from four workspaces down the left and their tasks across backlog, active and blocked lanes, beside a sidebar of workspaces with their services as dots",
      pos: 'left top',
    },
    caption: 'One supervisor per window: rules you write watch every agent, and the board shows every workstream at once.',
  },
  { id: 'mesh', title: 'Mesh workspace', blurb: 'Specialists that ask each other' },
  { id: 'mailink', title: 'maiLink companion', blurb: 'Your agents in your pocket, no cloud' },
  { id: 'comms', title: 'Chat threads', blurb: 'Answer where the report landed' },
  { id: 'follow-ups', title: 'Follow-ups', blurb: 'Agents that come back on their own' },
];

/** The rows that are tabs for the hero picture, in page order, and the one shown first. */
const SHOT_ROWS = KEY_FEATURES.filter((f) => f.shot);
const DEFAULT_SHOT = 'overlord';

const EVERYTHING = [
  {
    title: 'Restart without losing anything',
    body: 'Reboot the machine and every tab comes back as it was — scrollback, layout, SSH sessions, agent sessions — each resumed agent reconnected to its tab with nothing typed by anyone, and the dev servers you left running started again.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Know what is in every tab',
    body: 'Interpolated names like <code>CustomName %title</code> combine your label with live terminal state. No more guessing across fifteen identical “bash” tabs.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Workspaces per project',
    body: 'Group terminals by project, each with its own pane layout and context. Suspend the inactive ones to free resources; they resume with full state. Move a workspace or a single tab to another window and everything in it keeps running — or move the project folder itself, and its tabs, services and agents’ sessions and memory follow it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Hand a workspace to a teammate',
    body: 'Share a workspace as one file. Whoever opens it gets the same tabs in the same repositories — cloned for them where they don’t have them, services and agents included — and none of your accounts, scrollback or secrets. On their side, every command it would run is listed word for word first, and only the ones they approve ever run.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Bridge two agents',
    body: 'Connect two running sessions — one local, one over SSH, a Claude Code beside a Codex or an Antigravity — so they ask each other questions directly, in a split pane you can watch.',
  },
  {
    title: 'A composer, not a shell line',
    body: 'A multi-line dock under every terminal. Enter makes newlines, <code>Cmd+Enter</code> sends, drafts survive restarts, and pasted screenshots become attachments — uploaded automatically over SSH.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Built-in editor',
    body: 'Click any file path in terminal output to open it in an editor tab — over SSH too, loaded transparently. 50+ languages, with an inline merge view when a file changes under your unsaved edits.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Deshittify your agent',
    body: 'One Preferences section switches off the parts of a coding agent that serve its vendor: telemetry, error uploads, feedback nags, Remote Control (off by default), other people’s comments on your claude.ai artifacts arriving as prompts (ignored by default), the fullscreen renderer that keeps a session out of your scrollback (classic UI kept by default), and the <code>Co-Authored-By</code> trailer in your history.',
  },
  {
    // v3.18.0. Backed by features/agent-defaults.md, checked against src-tauri/src/agent_defaults.rs
    // and components/agentDefaults/AgentDefaultsPane.svelte. Only TICKED settings are managed;
    // hosts need python3 — don't promise "every machine" without the tick.
    title: 'Every agent’s defaults, one place',
    body: 'Set what every session starts with — Claude Code’s model, effort and advisor, Codex’s model, reasoning effort and whether it keeps its conversation in your scrollback, Antigravity’s mode — written into each agent’s own settings file. Tick a setting and every SSH host you bridge to follows it, removals included; an unticked one is never touched.',
  },
  {
    // v3.18.0. Backed by features/apple-mail.md, checked against src-tauri/src/mail/ and
    // components/mail/MailPane.svelte. macOS only, off by default, ticked accounts only, never
    // Hive workers. Read-only — never say an agent can send or reply.
    title: 'Agents that can read your mail',
    body: 'On a Mac, let agents search and read the mail you already have in Apple Mail — a message, a whole thread, an attachment — for the accounts you tick. Read-only: nothing is sent, moved or marked read, and it stays off until you turn it on.',
  },
  {
    title: 'A Rust terminal underneath',
    body: 'alacritty_terminal does VTE parsing, buffering and scrollback in the backend, so the frontend only ever paints one screen. Scrollback persists to SQLite, crash-safe.',
  },
];
---

<Landing title={title} description={description} path="/">
  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- Hero */}
  <section class="ln-band ln-band--flush hero">
    <div class="ln-inner">
      <p class="ln-eyebrow">A terminal for multi-agent work</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">Keep a window full of agents on track</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">
        maiTerm runs Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity and your shells side by side — then shares a real
        kanban board with them, supervises them against rules you write, and brings every tab
        back exactly as it was. No LLM inside, no chat sidebar.
      </p>

      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="ln-btn ln-btn--primary ln-btn--lg" href="/download/">Download maiTerm</a>
        <a class="ln-btn ln-btn--ghost ln-btn--lg" href="/guides/getting-started/">Get started</a>
      </div>
      {/* The strongest honest thing we can say to someone who has never heard of us, and
          it was a grey footnote: Warp wants an account, Solo wants $99/yr past four
          projects. Give it the weight it earns. */}
      <ul class="hero-facts">
        <li><strong>Free</strong> — every feature, no paid tier</li>
        <li><strong>No account</strong> — nothing to sign up for</li>
        <li><strong>Source published</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="ln-note hero-note">macOS, Windows and Linux.</p>
    </div>

    {/* The desktop fold used to be one screenshot with a single card floating over
        it, and the whitespace either side of that card was doing nothing. A visitor
        who has never heard of us cannot tell from one picture of a kanban board
        what this product is for, and the answer was nine bands down — past the
        point where the people who needed convincing had already left.

        So the fold now carries the page's own contents list beside the shot: what
        maiTerm does, newest at the top with the weight it earns, a few rows with a
        line each, and the rest as a run of plain links so the list ends with the shot.
        It is a rail rather than a grid because a grid of seven would compete with
        the h1 for the eye; a single narrow column reads as an index, which is what
        it is.

        Below 64rem it stacks, and the rail goes FIRST — it is the content, the
        screenshot is the atmosphere. */}
    <div class="hero-lower" data-hero-tabs>
      <nav class="hero-rail" aria-label="What maiTerm does">
        {
          KEY_FEATURES.filter((f) => f.new).map((f) => (
            <a class="rail-item rail-item--new" href={`#${f.id}`}>
              <span class="rail-tag">New</span>
              <span class="rail-text">
                <strong>{f.title}</strong>
                <span class="rail-blurb">{f.blurb}</span>
              </span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
        {
          SHOT_ROWS.map((f) => (
            <a
              class="rail-item rail-item--shot"
              href={`#${f.id}`}
              data-shot={f.id}
              aria-current={f.id === DEFAULT_SHOT ? 'true' : undefined}
            >
              <span class="rail-text">
                <strong>{f.title}</strong>
                <span class="rail-blurb">{f.blurb}</span>
              </span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
        <ul class="rail-more">
          {
            KEY_FEATURES.filter((f) => !f.new && !f.shot).map((f) => (
              <li><a href={`#${f.id}`}>{f.title}</a></li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </nav>

      <div class="hero-shot">
        <div class="ln-shot">
          {
            SHOT_ROWS.map((f) => (
              <img
                src={f.shot.src}
                alt={f.shot.alt}
                width="2400"
                height="1500"
                data-shot={f.id}
                class:list={{ 'is-shown': f.id === DEFAULT_SHOT }}
                style={`object-position: ${f.shot.pos}`}
                fetchpriority={f.id === DEFAULT_SHOT ? 'high' : undefined}
                loading={f.id === DEFAULT_SHOT ? undefined : 'lazy'}
              />
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <div class="hero-caption" aria-live="polite">
          {
            SHOT_ROWS.map((f) => (
              <p data-shot={f.id} hidden={f.id !== DEFAULT_SHOT}>
                <span>{f.caption}</span>
                <a class="ln-cta-link" href={`#${f.id}`}>Read more about {f.title}</a>
              </p>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <script>
      // Desktop only: beside the picture the rows are its tabs. Pointing at or focusing one
      // shows its screenshot and caption; a click picks it rather than leaving the fold, and
      // the caption's Read more is the way down. Below 64rem the rows stay plain links to
      // their bands, which carry the pictures themselves.
      const root = document.querySelector('[data-hero-tabs]');
      if (root) {
        const wide = window.matchMedia('(min-width: 64rem)');
        const rows = [...root.querySelectorAll('.rail-item--shot')];
        const show = (id) => {
          for (const r of rows) {
            if (r.dataset.shot === id) r.setAttribute('aria-current', 'true');
            else r.removeAttribute('aria-current');
          }
          for (const img of root.querySelectorAll('img[data-shot]')) {
            img.classList.toggle('is-shown', img.dataset.shot === id);
          }
          for (const p of root.querySelectorAll('.hero-caption p[data-shot]')) {
            p.hidden = p.dataset.shot !== id;
          }
        };
        for (const r of rows) {
          r.addEventListener('pointerenter', () => { if (wide.matches) show(r.dataset.shot); });
          r.addEventListener('focus', () => { if (wide.matches) show(r.dataset.shot); });
          r.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
            if (!wide.matches) return;
            e.preventDefault();
            show(r.dataset.shot);
          });
        }
      }
    </script>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------- Antigravity */}
  {/* The release slot (v3.16.0): the hero rail's top entry links here. --raised, right after the
      --flush hero; inserting it flipped every band down to the --sunken compat strip.

      Every claim here is backed by features/agents.md "Antigravity" and checked against the code:
      - Google's Antigravity CLI, `agy` (descriptor.ts displayName 'Antigravity'). It maps onto the
        runtime once stubbed as Gemini CLI (wire key "gemini"); never call it Gemini here.
      - The plugin (claude_code/agy.rs) carries hooks, a rules file and the skill; MCP through the
        `--mcp-stdio` bridge. All four gemini_* preferences default ON (state/workspace.rs).
      - Permission prompt + trust dialog are READ OFF THE SCREEN (mailink/permission.rs, trust.rs):
        agy has no permission hook. Draft hold reads its `>` box (mailink/input_box.rs).
      - Resume: `agy --conversation %geminiSessionId` (resume.ts). No fork (supportsFork: false).
      - Proxied MCP servers locally and over ssh (mcp_proxy/entries.rs, ssh_tunnel.rs
        build_agy_setup_script — needs python3 on the host).
      - Hive workers are Claude Code only (features/hive.md) — the note says so. */}
  <section class="ln-band ln-band--raised" id="antigravity">
    <div class="ln-inner">
      <p class="ln-eyebrow">Antigravity</p>
      <h2 class="ln-h2 ln-h2--wide">Antigravity joins Claude Code and Codex.</h2>
      <p class="ln-lead">
        Run Google's Antigravity CLI, <code>agy</code>, in a maiTerm tab and it is a full agent,
        not a program in a box. maiTerm installs its plugin, so the tab gets maiTerm's tools and
        reports when it is working, idle or asking — in the sidebar, in the Loom and on your
        phone, with its chat, its model and how much of its context it has used.
      </p>

      <div class="ln-cards">
        <div class="ln-card">
          <h3>Everything the others get</h3>
          <p>
            The shared kanban board, follow-ups, mesh and bridge peers, and the MCP servers you
            signed in to once in maiTerm — on this Mac and on your SSH hosts.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ln-card">
          <h3>Stopped? You see where</h3>
          <p>
            Its permission prompts — for a tool or a shell command — and its workspace-trust dialog are read off the screen and wait
            for you, answerable from the Loom or your phone. Nothing maiTerm sends it is typed
            over a message you are still writing.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ln-card">
          <h3>Back after a restart</h3>
          <p>
            A restored tab resumes the same conversation with nothing typed by you, and the
            subagents it hands work to show in its chat, with their progress.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="ln-note band-note">
        On by default under Preferences → AI Agents; it takes effect once you run <code>agy</code>.
        Over SSH the host needs <code>python3</code>. A Hive's workers are still Claude Code.
      </p>
      <a class="ln-cta-link" href="/features/agents/#antigravity">How maiTerm runs Antigravity</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ----------------------------------------------------------------- Hive */}
  {/* The release slot until v3.16.0 (v3.13.0). Plain since v3.16.0's Antigravity band went in
      above it, which flipped every band down to the --sunken compat strip.

      Every claim here is backed by features/hive.md and checked against the code:
      - The coordinator is "Queen" on screen (create.rs COORDINATOR_TAB_NAME); she is NOT
        sandboxed (settings.rs) — only the workers are. Don't say "every agent is sandboxed".
      - Worktree per worker per git folder (worktree.rs); lands are the reviewed commits, after
        the land check, with a log and Undo as a revert commit (land/, LandLogModal.svelte).
      - Workers' prompts/questions go to the Queen first (prompts.rs, autonomy.ts); some always
        come to the human. Precedents are recorded by the Queen from the human's own answers.
      - Completion criteria: no worker starts until the human confirms them (finish.rs).
      - Hosts: the Queen grants them (change.rs), not the human. v3.17.0: hosts, sockets and tools
        sit behind the "What workers may use" checkpoint, the Queen's by default
        (change_plan.rs route); "can grant" because a human can set it to Ask me. The worker
        check is 15 min by default, 0 off (hive/health.rs).
      - Windows refused (create.rs); queued jobs macOS-only (job_sandbox.rs) — hence "macOS".
      - v3.14.0 media: the capabilities are the catalogue's (resources/hive/media/catalogue.json);
        setup is a sign-in, a pasted key or a Homebrew install (lib/hive/mediaSetup.ts), and Verify
        spends nothing (hive/media/verify.rs). Remotion and Google genmedia are guide-only — don't
        say "every provider sets up in place". */}
  <section class="ln-band" id="hive">
    <div class="ln-inner">
      <p class="ln-eyebrow">Hive</p>
      <h2 class="ln-h2 ln-h2--wide">Give the goal to a Queen. She runs the crew.</h2>
      <p class="ln-lead">
        Running a dozen agents on one goal means being their scheduler, their reviewer and the
        person who merges what they did. Make a workspace a <strong>Hive</strong> and an agent
        does that job: the Queen plans the work in waves, starts sandboxed Claude Code workers
        on it, answers their questions, and lands their work once it passes its check.
      </p>
      <p class="ln-lead">
        You confirm what <em>done</em> means before any worker starts, and watch the whole thing
        on one screen: the Queen's chat, her running estimate of how far the goal has come, a
        board of workers with what each is doing right now, and any worker's chat beside it.
      </p>

      <div class="ln-cards">
        <div class="ln-card">
          <h3>A branch each, landed after a check</h3>
          <p>
            In a git repository every worker has its own copy on its own branch. Finished work
            lands back after a land check you or the Queen set, with a log of every land and an Undo that
            reverts rather than rewriting history.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ln-card">
          <h3>Asked only what she can't decide</h3>
          <p>
            Workers' permission prompts and questions go to the Queen first. When one needs a
            website, a local database or a tool, she can grant it herself, and every 15 minutes
            she checks on any worker that may be stuck. What she brings you waits in one
            <strong>Needs you</strong> list, and you can take over anything she's holding.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ln-card">
          <h3>Not only code</h3>
          <p>
            See what a Hive can make on this Mac — video, images, voice, music, 3D — and set up
            what it lacks without leaving maiTerm: a sign-in, a key or an install, then a check
            that spends nothing. What it makes lands in a gallery with how each piece was made,
            and every agent shares one memory you can read and edit.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="ln-note band-note">
        Claude Code 2.1.295 or newer, on macOS. Workers run in Claude Code's sandbox and reach
        only the network hosts the Queen grants them.
      </p>
      <a class="ln-cta-link" href="/features/hive/">How a Hive works</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ---------------------------------------------------------------- Files */}
  {/* The release slot until v3.13.0 (v3.12.0). Plain after the Hive band went in above it;
      --raised since v3.16.0's Antigravity band flipped the page again.

      Every claim here is backed by features/files.md and checked against the code:
      - Reads light briefly, changes keep a dot in the agent's colour for the turn (agents.ts,
        TreeBody.svelte); a shell call's writes are labelled "while Bash ran", never "edited".
